From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:05:53 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132: Add CPU cluster performance
 controllers

Describe the two T8132 (M4) CPU cluster performance controllers
(DVFS_CMD blocks of the E and P clusters at 0x210e20000 and
0x211e20000) and their operating point tables, and point every CPU
node at its cluster's controller and OPP table.

The controller nodes are left disabled: on T8132 the
apple-soc-cpufreq driver caps the cluster at the unmanaged P-state
limit until a board thermal owner releases it, so boards opt in
individually once such an owner exists for them. With the controllers
disabled this change has no runtime effect on any T8132 board.

The register windows come from the pmgr "reg" entries of the J604,
J713 and J773g ADTs (identical across all of them). The frequency and
voltage levels come from the pmgr voltage-states1 (E cluster, 8
states) and voltage-states5 (P cluster, 20 states) properties of the
J604 runtime ADT under macOS 27.2 (26B123). The two highest P-cluster
states are marked turbo-mode, matching other Apple SoC trees.

No opp-microwatt or capacity-dmips-mhz values are added here: the
values published so far were measured on J713 and have not been
verified on other T8132 boards.

This follows the same layout as earlier T8132 cpufreq work by
Eryk Wieliczko (aurora-silicon/linux c6b301adfd) and by Niklas Sheth
and Sean Dolbec (techmore/linux-aurora 20591b9f31), whose frequency
tables match the J604 ADT data.

Link: https://github.com/aurora-silicon/linux/commit/c6b301adfd
Link: https://github.com/techmore/linux-aurora/commit/20591b9f31
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
